🔄 How services communicate without Eureka
When you are not using Eureka (or another service registry), services must know each other’s address somehow.

Common options:


✅ 1. Hardcoded URLs/IPs
Each service knows the other service address in code.

```java
// UserService calling OrderService
String url = "http://localhost:8082/orders";
ResponseEntity<String> orders = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, String.class);
```

Works only if address is fixed.

Breaks when:
- host/port changes
- multiple instances exist (need manual LB)
- environments differ (dev/stage/prod)


✅ 2. Environment / config properties
Keep URLs in `application.yml`:

```yaml
order-service:
  url: http://localhost:8082
```

Better than hardcoding in Java, but still manual:
- update URLs per environment
- no auto-registration when instances appear/disappear


✅ 3. API Gateway with static routes
Use Spring Cloud Gateway / NGINX with fixed upstream URLs:

```yaml
spring:
  cloud:
    gateway:
      routes:
        - id: order-service
          uri: http://localhost:8082
          predicates:
            - Path=/orders/**
```

Still manual routing config.
(Note: Gateway can also use Eureka discovery — that is the “with Eureka” style.)


✅ 4. Docker Compose / Kubernetes naming
Docker Compose example:

```yaml
services:
  userservice:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8081:8081"
  orderservice:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "8082:8082"
```

Call by container/service name:
```text
http://orderservice:8082/orders
```

Important nuance:
- Docker Compose resolves names via **Docker DNS** (still DNS, just local to the compose network)
- Kubernetes has its own Service/DNS discovery (often replaces Eureka)
So “without Eureka” does not always mean “no discovery at all”.


❌ Typical pain without a registry (like Eureka)
- Manual URL/IP maintenance
- Hard to add/remove instances dynamically
- Load balancing must be configured separately (Gateway/NGINX/K8s/etc.)
- No central “who is up right now?” registry view


✅ What Eureka adds
| Feature            | Without Eureka                         | With Eureka                                      |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Service addresses  | Hardcoded / config / static gateway    | Discover by service name                         |
| Auto-discovery     | ❌ (unless K8s/other)                   | ✅                                                |
| Multi-instance LB  | Manual (NGINX/Gateway/K8s)             | Client-side LB (`@LoadBalanced`) + instance list |
| Scaling instances  | Manual config updates often needed     | New instances register themselves                |
| Health awareness   | Depends on your setup                  | Heartbeat + registry eviction (with caveats)     |


🔁 Interview final line:
Without Eureka, services usually communicate via hardcoded URLs, config properties, or static gateway routes — hard to scale and maintain.
Eureka provides dynamic registration + discovery by service name.


