🧠 "order-service is resolved by Eureka, not DNS" — meaning

🌐 What is DNS resolution?
DNS = Domain Name System.
It converts a hostname to an IP.

Example:
`google.com` → DNS → `142.250.190.78`


🔴 Without Eureka (+ no @LoadBalanced discovery client)
Code:
```java
String url = "http://order-service/api/orders";
```

Java treats `order-service` as a normal hostname and asks OS/DNS:
- hosts file
- local/network DNS

If not found → `UnknownHostException`.


🟢 With Eureka + `@LoadBalanced`
```java
@LoadBalanced
@Bean
public RestTemplate restTemplate() {
    return new RestTemplate();
}

restTemplate.getForEntity("http://order-service/api/orders", String.class);
```

Spring intercepts the hostname `order-service` and does NOT use OS DNS for it.
Instead:
1) Look up `order-service` in Eureka registry
2) Get instance list (e.g. 192.168.1.10:8082 , 192.168.1.11:8082)
3) LoadBalancer picks one instance
4) Call that instance

So:
`http://order-service` → resolved by Eureka + LoadBalancer, not by OS DNS.


✅ What you need for this to work
```yaml
spring:
  application:
    name: user-service

eureka:
  client:
    service-url:
      defaultZone: http://localhost:8761/eureka/
```

And a `@LoadBalanced` RestTemplate/WebClient.


🧾 Summary
| Without Eureka                         | With Eureka + @LoadBalanced                      |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ |
| Hostname resolved by DNS/hosts         | Service id resolved via Eureka registry          |
| Often need real host/IP in URL         | Can use logical name (`order-service`)           |
| LB is separate/manual                  | Client-side LB picks an instance                 |

Interview one-liner:
With `@LoadBalanced`, `http://order-service` is a Eureka service id, not a DNS hostname.
